      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "____  \n  \n| - | We know that the product of two numbers equals the product of their least common multiple and their greatest common divisor.  \nLet the least common multiple be denoted by \\(M\\) and the greatest common divisor be \\(D\\).  \nGiven: \\(M = 36\\) and \\(D = 6\\).  \nWe can express this as:\n\\[ N_1 \\times D_1 = M \\times D \\]\nwhere \\(N_1\\) and \\(D_1\\) are the two unknown numbers.  \nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[ N_1 \\times D_1 = 36 \\times 6 \\]\n\n| | Now, let's express \\(N_1\\) and \\(D_1\\) as:\n$$ N_1 = 6M_1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad  D_1 = 6D_1 $$\n\nSolution:\nWe use the transformations \\(N_1 = kd_1 \\times 6\\) and \\( D_1 = kd_1 \\times 6\\), with \\(d_1\\) being \\(D\\).  \nSubstituting back:\n\\[ \\text{P} = M \\times D = 36 \\times 6 \\cdot d_1 \\]\n\nGiven that the problem provides the least common multiple and the greatest common divisor directly, we've used their product as \\(6M_1d_1\\) correctly, where we recognize that \\(M_1\\) (\"M1\") denotes M divided by the greatest common divisor, not multiplied by the greatest common divisor again.\n\nThe products of \\(M\\) and \\(D\\) so determined correspond to:\n\\[ \\text{P} = 216 \\]\nThe product of the two numbers is therefore:\n\\[ \\boxed{216} \\]",
