      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the property of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM). We are given that the LCM of two numbers is 36 and their GCD is 6. Let's call the two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nRecall the relationship between the LCM and GCD of two numbers:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b \\]\n\nLet's plug in the given values:\n\\[ 36 \\times 6 = a \\times b \\]\n\\[ 216 = a \\times b \\]\n\nNext, we need to consider the prime factorizations of 36 and 6:\n\\[ 36 = 2^2 \\times 3^2 \\]\n\\[ 6 = 2 \\times 3 \\]\n\nUsing the property of GCD, we express the number 6 as:\n\\[ 6 = 2^1 \\times 3^1 \\]\n\nAnd the number 36 as:\n\\[ 36 = 2^2 \\times 3^2 \\]\n\nUsing the prime factors 2 and 3, we know:\n\\[ a = 6 \\times 2^2 \\times 3^0 \\] (since 2 and 3 come from the GCD of 16)\n\\[ b = 6 \\times 2^2 \\times 3^1 \\]\n\nThen:\n\\[ a = 6 \\times 4 \\times 1 = 24 \\]\n\\[ b = 6 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 72 \\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the product of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[ a \\times b = 24 \\times 72 \\]\n\nWe can verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Given values\r\ngcd = 6\r\nlcm = 36\r\n\r\n# Calculate the product of the two numbers\r\nproduct_ab = lcm * gcd\r\nprint(product_ab)\n```\n```output\n216\n```\nThe product of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{216}\\).",
